Nocturnal hypoglycemia (NH) is a relevant complication that affects people with Type 1 Diabetes (T1D) during sleep,
which occurs when glucose falls below 70mgl/dL. NH usually remains unnoticed, making it difficult to perform preventive
actions. Continuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM) systems are designed to control glucose measurements over time, helping
to maintain target levels. Despite the CGM advances, controlling both glucose and glycemic events remains challenging.
Artificial Intelligence (AI) models are emerged as promising approaches for identifying CGM patterns associated with NH
and supporting its prediction.


The main objective of this end-of-degree project is to evaluate the effectiveness of an artificial neural network model
named Laplace-Approximated Neural Additive Model (LA-NAM) for predicting NH and identifying its most relevant factors.
LA-NAM was compared with Logistic Regression(LR), Random Forest (RF), XGBoost, and Support Vector Classifier (SVC) to
assess its ability to obtain competitive performance while providing interpretability. We also evaluate the effectiveness
of combining temporal windows with the extraction of range of CGM-derived features, and their impact on NH prediction.
We apply undersampling and cost-sensitive methods for class imbalance, and perform a feature pruning approach. We used a
cohort of 41 patients with T1D from the Complejo Hospitalario Universitario Insular Materno-Infantil de Las Palmas
de Gran Canaria. The glucose recordings were transformed into clinically relevant variables using: statistical descriptors,
glucose-risk indexes, variability measurements, time-in-range metrics, observation-based variables, and glycemic control indicators.
